course: dictionary
lesson: 4969
title: Include
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To include means to have something as one of the parts of a whole, or to put someone or something into a group. Look at «The price includes a drink.» The drink is already one of the parts of the price, so it is counted in the total.
explain: When you include someone, you make sure they are not left out. «Include everyone in the game» means invite every single person to join in. Ask yourself: is something already part of the whole, or am I adding it to the group?
explain: «Including» means and also, counting this one. In «A prank can be fun if everyone laughs together, including the person tricked», the person tricked is one of the people who laughs. Swap in and also and the sentence still makes sense.
fact: To include means to have something as one of the parts of a whole.
fact: To include someone means to put them in a group, so they are not left out.
fact: «Including» means and also, counting this one.
fact: Useful phrases: «include in», «includes everything», and «service included».
fact: In writing, you can include details and examples to make your ideas clearer.
exercise: «The price includes a drink.» What does includes mean here? | *Has the drink as one of its parts | Forgets the drink | Costs more than the drink | Throws away the drink
exercise: Mia says, «Let's include Sam in the game.» What does include mean? | Ask Sam to pay | *Put Sam in the group | Send Sam home | Hide Sam from the others
exercise: «Recommendations may push videos that keep people watching, including angry or scary ones.» What does including mean here? | Before angry ones | Without angry or scary ones | *And also, counting angry or scary ones | Instead of angry or scary ones
test: What is the best way to include a new child at lunch? | Ignore them | *Invite them to sit with you | Laugh at their accent | Tell them to go away
test: Which sentence uses include to mean have as one of the parts? | *Your ticket includes a free snack | Please include your name on the form | Let's include Sam in the game | Include everyone in the team
exam: Which word means and also, counting this one? | *including | barely | ignoring | lapsed
